      "subSubject": "History of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "According to _____: \"By 7,000 years ago agriculture was firmly established in Baluchistan",
      "answer": "Tim Dyson",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " By this is meant circumstances where a subgroup—such as a tribe—derives from a tiny number of 'original' individuals.  Further, compared to most world regions, the subcontinent's people are relatively distinct in having practised comparatively high levels of endogamy.  === Neolithic === Settled life emerged on the subcontinent in the western margins of the Indus River alluvium approximately 9,000 years ago, evolving gradually into the Indus Valley Civilisation of the third millennium BCE.  According to Tim Dyson: \"By 7,000 years ago agriculture was firmly established in Baluchistan.  [and] slowly spread eastwards into the Indus valley. \" Michael Fisher adds: The earliest discovered instance .  of well-established, settled agricultural society is at Mehrgarh in the hills between the Bolan Pass and the Indus plain (today in Pakistan) (see Map 3"
    },
    {
      "id": "q82",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Indian History",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:27:18.163Z",
      "subject": "Indian History",
      "subSubject": "History of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "\" _____ adds: The earliest discovered instance",
      "answer": "Michael Fisher",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " === Neolithic === Settled life emerged on the subcontinent in the western margins of the Indus River alluvium approximately 9,000 years ago, evolving gradually into the Indus Valley Civilisation of the third millennium BCE.  According to Tim Dyson: \"By 7,000 years ago agriculture was firmly established in Baluchistan.  [and] slowly spread eastwards into the Indus valley. \" Michael Fisher adds: The earliest discovered instance .  of well-established, settled agricultural society is at Mehrgarh in the hills between the Bolan Pass and the Indus plain (today in Pakistan) (see Map 3.  From as early as 7000 BCE, communities there started investing increased labor in preparing the land and selecting, planting, tending, and harvesting particular grain-producing plants.  They also domesticated animals, including sheep, goats, pigs, and oxen (both humped zebu [Bos indicus] and unhumped [Bos taurus])"
    },
    {
      "id": "q83",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Indian History",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:27:18.163Z",
      "subject": "Indian History",
      "subSubject": "History of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "of well-established, settled agricultural society is at Mehrgarh in the hills between the _____ and the Indus plain (today in Pakistan) (see Map 3",
      "answer": "Bolan Pass",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " According to Tim Dyson: \"By 7,000 years ago agriculture was firmly established in Baluchistan.  [and] slowly spread eastwards into the Indus valley. \" Michael Fisher adds: The earliest discovered instance .  of well-established, settled agricultural society is at Mehrgarh in the hills between the Bolan Pass and the Indus plain (today in Pakistan) (see Map 3.  From as early as 7000 BCE, communities there started investing increased labor in preparing the land and selecting, planting, tending, and harvesting particular grain-producing plants.  They also domesticated animals, including sheep, goats, pigs, and oxen (both humped zebu [Bos indicus] and unhumped [Bos taurus]).  Castrating oxen, for instance, turned them from mainly meat sources into domesticated draft-animals as well"
    },
    {
