      "answer": "King Bimbisara",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Their administrations were divided into executive, judicial, and military functions.  Early sources, from the Buddhist Pāli Canon, the Jain Agamas and the Hindu Puranas, mention Magadha being ruled by the Pradyota dynasty and Haryanka dynasty (c.  544–413 BCE) for some 200 years, c.  King Bimbisara of the Haryanka dynasty led an active and expansive policy, conquering Anga in what is now eastern Bihar and West Bengal.  King Bimbisara was overthrown and killed by his son, Prince Ajatashatru, who continued the expansionist policy of Magadha.  During this period, Gautama Buddha, the founder of Buddhism, lived much of his life in the Magadha kingdom.  He attained enlightenment in Bodh Gaya, gave his first sermon in Sarnath and the first Buddhist council was held in Rajgriha"
