      "subSubject": "History of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "The murder of the _____ Conference leader Allah Bakhsh Soomro also made it easier for the pro-separatist All-India Muslim League to demand the creation of a Pakistan",
      "answer": "All India Azad Muslim",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The All India Azad Muslim Conference gathered in Delhi in April 1940 to voice its support for an independent and united India.  Its members included several Islamic organisations in India, as well as 1,400 nationalist Muslim delegates.  The pro-separatist All-India Muslim League worked to try to silence those nationalist Muslims who stood against the partition of India, often using \"intimidation and coercion\".  The murder of the All India Azad Muslim Conference leader Allah Bakhsh Soomro also made it easier for the pro-separatist All-India Muslim League to demand the creation of a Pakistan.  ==== After World War II (c.  1946–1947) ==== In January 1946, several mutinies broke out in the armed services, starting with that of RAF servicemen frustrated with their slow repatriation.  The mutinies came to a head with mutiny of the Royal Indian Navy in Bombay in February 1946, followed by others in Calcutta, Madras, and Karachi"
    },
    {
      "id": "q311",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Indian History",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:27:18.337Z",
