      "question": "According to _____, Jahangir and Shah Jahan intended to build observatories too, but were unable to do so",
      "answer": "Sulaiman Nadvi",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The new curriculum produced a series of eminent scholars, engineers and architects.  === Astronomy === While there appears to have been little concern for theoretical astronomy, Mughal astronomers made advances in observational astronomy and produced some Zij treatises.  Humayun built a personal observatory near Delhi.  According to Sulaiman Nadvi, Jahangir and Shah Jahan intended to build observatories too, but were unable to do so.  The astronomical instruments and observational techniques used at the Mughal observatories were mainly derived from Islamic astronomy.  In the 17th century, the Mughal Empire saw a synthesis between Islamic and Hindu astronomy, where Islamic observational instruments were combined with Hindu computational techniques.  During the decline of the Mughal Empire, the Hindu king Jai Singh II of Amber continued the work of Mughal astronomy"
    },
