      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Although unable to deter Vietnam from ousting Pol Pot, China demonstrated that its Cold War communist adversary, the Soviet Union, was unable to protect its Vietnamese ally.  Secretary of State Henry Kissinger wrote that \"China succeeded in exposing the limits of. [Soviet] strategic reach\" and speculated that the desire to \"compensate for their ineffectuality\" contributed to the Soviets' decision to intervene in Afghanistan a year later.  In the 1973 oil crisis, Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) cut their petroleum output.  This raised oil prices and hurt Western economies, but helped the Soviet Union by generating a huge flow of money from its oil sales.  As a result of the oil crisis, combined with the growing influence of Third World alignments, such as OPEC and the Non-Aligned Movement, less powerful countries had more room to assert their independence and often showed themselves resistant to pressure from either superpower.  Meanwhile, Moscow was forced to turn its attention inward to deal with the Soviet Union's deep-seated domestic economic problems"
