      "subSubject": "Culture of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Odia's importance to Indian culture, from ancient times, is evidenced by its presence in Ashoka's _____ X, dated to the 2nd century BC",
      "answer": "Rock Edict",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The Ashoka rock edict found at Brahmagiri (dated 230 BCE) has been suggested to contain a word in identifiable Kannada.  Odia is India's 6th classical language in addition to Sanskrit, Tamil, Telugu, Kannada, and Malayalam.  It is also one of the 22 official languages in the 8th schedule of Indian constitution.  Odia's importance to Indian culture, from ancient times, is evidenced by its presence in Ashoka's Rock Edict X, dated to the 2nd century BC.  The language with the largest number of speakers in India is Hindi and its various dialects.  Early forms of present-day Hindustani developed from the Middle Indo-Aryan apabhraṃśa vernaculars of present-day North India in the 7th–13th centuries.  During the time of Islamic rule in parts of India, it became influenced by Persian"
    },
    {
      "id": "q1128",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Art & Culture",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:27:41.621Z",
      "subject": "Art & Culture",
      "subSubject": "Culture of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Early forms of present-day Hindustani developed from the _____-Aryan apabhraṃśa vernaculars of present-day North India in the 7th–13th centuries",
      "answer": "Middle Indo",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " It is also one of the 22 official languages in the 8th schedule of Indian constitution.  Odia's importance to Indian culture, from ancient times, is evidenced by its presence in Ashoka's Rock Edict X, dated to the 2nd century BC.  The language with the largest number of speakers in India is Hindi and its various dialects.  Early forms of present-day Hindustani developed from the Middle Indo-Aryan apabhraṃśa vernaculars of present-day North India in the 7th–13th centuries.  During the time of Islamic rule in parts of India, it became influenced by Persian.  The Persian influence led to the development of Urdu, which is more Persianised and written in the Perso-Arabic script.  Modern standard Hindi has a lesser Persian influence and is written in the Devanagari script"
    },
    {
      "id": "q1129",
