      "answer": "Madhya Pradesh",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The average elevation of these hills is from 300 to 600 m (980 to 1,970 ft) and rarely goes above 700 metres (2,300 ft).  They are believed to have been formed by the wastes created by the weathering of the ancient Aravali mountains.  Geographically, it separates Northern India from Southern India.  The western end of the range lies in eastern Gujarat, near its border with Madhya Pradesh, and runs east and north, almost meeting the Ganges at Mirzapur.  Satpura Range, lies south of Vindhya range and east of Aravali range, begins in eastern Gujarat near the Arabian Sea coast and runs east across Maharashtra, Madhya Pradesh and Chhattisgarh.  It extends 900 km (560 mi) with many peaks rising above 1,000 m (3,300 ft).  It is triangular in shape, with its apex at Ratnapuri and the two sides being parallel to the Tapti and Narmada rivers"
