      "answer": "North India",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Some parts of the country with subtropical, temperate or continental climates also experience spring and autumn.  India's geography and geology are climatically pivotal: the Thar Desert in the northwest and the Himalayas in the north work in tandem to create a culturally and economically important monsoonal regime.  As Earth's highest and most massive mountain range, the Himalayas bar the influx of frigid katabatic winds from the icy Tibetan Plateau and northerly Central Asia.  Most of North India is thus kept warm or is only mildly chilly or cold during winter; the same thermal dam keeps most regions in India hot in summer.  The climate in South India is generally warmer, and more humid due to its coastlines.  However some hill stations in South India such as Ooty are well known for their cold climate.  Though the Tropic of Cancer—the boundary that is between the tropics and subtropics—passes through the middle of India, the bulk of the country can be regarded as climatically tropical"
