      "hint": "",
      "fact": " While India had become a republic, British officers seconded from the British Armed Forces continued to hold senior positions in the Indian Armed Forces into the early 1960s.  On 1 April 1954, Air Marshal Subroto Mukherjee became the first Indian Commander-in-Chief of the Indian Air Force.  Effective from 1 April 1955, a parliamentary act, the Commanders-In-Chiefs (Change in Designation) Act, re-designated the office of Commander-in-Chief as the Chief of Staff of each branch.  Not until 1958 would the last British chief of staff of the Indian Navy be succeeded by an Indian.  On 22 April of that year, Vice Admiral Ram Dass Katari became the first Indian Chief of Naval Staff.  The Chiefs of Staff of the Indian Air Force and the Indian Navy were upgraded to four-star rank on par with the Chief of Army Staff in 1966 and 1968, respectively.  In 1961, tensions rose between India and Portugal over the Portuguese-occupied territory of Goa, which India claimed for itself"
