      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The army has embarked on an infantry modernisation program known as Futuristic Infantry Soldier As a System (F-INSAS), and is also upgrading and acquiring new assets for its armoured, artillery, and aviation branches.  == History == Until the independence of India, the \"Indian Army\" was a British-commanded force defined as \"the force recruited locally and permanently based in India, together with its expatriate British officers\"; the \"British Army in India\" referred to British Army units posted to India for a tour of duty.  The \"Army of India\" meant the combined Indian Army and the British Army in India.  === Background === In 1776, a Military Department was created within the government of the East India Company at Calcutta.  Its main function was to record orders that were issued to the army by various departments of the East India Company for the territories under its control.  With the Charter Act 1833, the Secretariat of the government of the East India Company was reorganised into four departments, including a Military Department.  The army in the presidencies of Bengal, Bombay and Madras functioned as respective Presidency Armies until 1 April 1895, when they were unified i"
