      "subject": "Environment & Ecology",
      "subSubject": "Environment of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "The Indian Wildlife Protection Act _____ protected the biodiversity",
      "answer": "1972",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " === Policy and law === In the Directive Principles of State Policy, Article 48 says \"the state shall endeavour to protect and improve the environment and to safeguard the forests and wildlife of the country\"; Article 51-A states that \"it shall be the duty of every citizen of India to protect and improve the natural environment including forests, lakes, rivers and wildlife and to have compassion for living creatures. \" India is one of the parties of the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) treaty.  Prior to the CBD, India had different laws to govern the environment.  The Indian Wildlife Protection Act 1972 protected the biodiversity.  In addition to this act, the government passed the Environment (Protection) Act 1986 and Foreign Trade (Development and Regulation) Act 1992 for control of biodiversity.  === Renewable energy === Renewable energy in India comes under the purview of the Ministry of New and Renewable Energy.  India was the first country in the world to set up a ministry of non-conventional energy resources, in the early 1980s"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2485",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Environment & Ecology",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:29:35.986Z",
      "subject": "Environment & Ecology",
      "subSubject": "Environment of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "In addition to this act, the government passed the Environment (Protection) Act _____ and Foreign Trade (Development and Regulation) Act 1992 for control of biodiversity",
      "answer": "1986",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": "\" India is one of the parties of the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) treaty.  Prior to the CBD, India had different laws to govern the environment.  The Indian Wildlife Protection Act 1972 protected the biodiversity.  In addition to this act, the government passed the Environment (Protection) Act 1986 and Foreign Trade (Development and Regulation) Act 1992 for control of biodiversity.  === Renewable energy === Renewable energy in India comes under the purview of the Ministry of New and Renewable Energy.  India was the first country in the world to set up a ministry of non-conventional energy resources, in the early 1980s.  Its cumulative grid interactive or grid tied renewable energy capacity (excluding large hydro) has reached 203"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2486",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Environment & Ecology",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:29:35.986Z",
      "subject": "Environment & Ecology",
      "subSubject": "Environment of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "== Environmentalism == In _____, the government launched Project Tiger, a conservation program aimed at protecting the national animal, the tiger",
      "answer": "1973",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " India's initiatives such as green energy corridor and PM Surya ghar muft bijlii yojna promote renewable adoption.  The government allows 100% FDI in the sector and collaborates internationally (such as partnership with Australia) to boost investments.  Regardless of challenges like funding gap and skill shortages, India targets 500GW of renewable energy capacity by 2030.  == Environmentalism == In 1973, the government launched Project Tiger, a conservation program aimed at protecting the national animal, the tiger.  Its population reached as low as 2000 in 1970.  Human population growth, cultivation of forest land and mainly hunting were the key factors for this decline.  Aided by the World Wildlife Fund (WWF) and the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN), Indian conservationists were instrumental in getting the government to ban hunting and set aside national parks"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2487",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Environment & Ecology",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:29:35.987Z",
      "subject": "Environment & Ecology",
      "subSubject": "Environment of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "India has laws protecting the environment and is one of the countries that signed the Convention on _____ (CBD) treaty",
      "answer": "Biological Diversity",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": "The environment of India comprises some of the world's most biodiverse ecozones.  The Deccan Traps, Gangetic Plains and the Himalayas are the major geographical features.  The country faces different forms of pollution as its major environmental issue and is more vulnerable to the effects of climate change being a developing nation.  India has laws protecting the environment and is one of the countries that signed the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) treaty.  The Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change and each particular state forest departments plan and implement environmental policies throughout the country.  == Features == === Biota === India has some of the world's most biodiverse ecozones—desert, high mountains, highlands, tropical and temperate forests, swamplands, plains, grasslands, areas surrounding rivers and an island archipelago.  It hosts three biodiversity hotspots: the Western Ghats, the Himalayas and the Indo-Burma region"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2488",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Environment & Ecology",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:29:35.987Z",
      "subject": "Environment & Ecology",
      "subSubject": "Environment of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "It hosts three biodiversity hotspots: the _____, the Himalayas and the Indo-Burma region",
      "answer": "Western Ghats",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " India has laws protecting the environment and is one of the countries that signed the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) treaty.  The Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change and each particular state forest departments plan and implement environmental policies throughout the country.  == Features == === Biota === India has some of the world's most biodiverse ecozones—desert, high mountains, highlands, tropical and temperate forests, swamplands, plains, grasslands, areas surrounding rivers and an island archipelago.  It hosts three biodiversity hotspots: the Western Ghats, the Himalayas and the Indo-Burma region.  These hotspots have numerous endemic species.  In 2021, around 7,13,789km2 of land in the country was under forests and 92 percent of that belonged to the government. 7 percent was forested compared to the recommended 33 percent by the National Forest Policy Resolution (1952)"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2489",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Environment & Ecology",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:29:35.987Z",
      "subject": "Environment & Ecology",
      "subSubject": "Environment of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "7 percent was forested compared to the recommended 33 percent by the _____ (1952)",
      "answer": "National Forest Policy Resolution",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " It hosts three biodiversity hotspots: the Western Ghats, the Himalayas and the Indo-Burma region.  These hotspots have numerous endemic species.  In 2021, around 7,13,789km2 of land in the country was under forests and 92 percent of that belonged to the government. 7 percent was forested compared to the recommended 33 percent by the National Forest Policy Resolution (1952).  Majority of it are broad-leaved deciduous trees which comprise one-sixth sal and one-tenth teak.  Coniferous types are found in the northern high altitude regions and comprise pines, junipers and deodars.  There are 350 species of mammals, 375 reptiles, 130 amphibians, 20,000 insects, 19000 fish and 1200 species of birds in India"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2490",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Environment & Ecology",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:29:35.987Z",
      "subject": "Environment & Ecology",
      "subSubject": "Environment of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Elephants, the _____ and eight species of deer are also found",
      "answer": "Indian Rhinoceros",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Coniferous types are found in the northern high altitude regions and comprise pines, junipers and deodars.  There are 350 species of mammals, 375 reptiles, 130 amphibians, 20,000 insects, 19000 fish and 1200 species of birds in India.  The Asiatic lion, Bengal tiger and leopard are the main predators; the country has the most species of cats than any other.  Elephants, the Indian Rhinoceros and eight species of deer are also found.  There are over 17000 species of flowering plants in India, which account for six percent of the total plant species in the world.  India comprises seven percent of world's flora.  Wide range of climatic conditions in India gave rise to rich variety of flora"
    },
    {
