      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:29:35.990Z",
      "subject": "Environment & Ecology",
      "subSubject": "Climate change in India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "What is Emissions, effects and responses of India related to climate change?",
      "answer": "Climate change in India",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " While India, the US and China are the world's largest carbon polluters, the social cost of carbon is only US$50 per tonne for the US and US$26 per tonne for China.  A report by the London-based global think tank Overseas Development Institute found that India may lose anywhere around 3–10% of its GDP annually by 2100 and its poverty rate may rise by 3. 5% in 2040 due to climate change.  ==== Reduced crop yields ==== Climate change in India will have disproportionate ramifications for the more than 400 million that make up India's poor community.  This is because so many depend on natural resources for their food, shelter and income.  Indian farmers are the first to feel the economic effects of climate change since 85% of Indian farmers have low financial resilience.  More than 56% of people in India work in agriculture, while many others earn their living in coastal areas"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2514",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Environment & Ecology",
      "region": "",
