      "subSubject": "Foreign relations of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "The US provided support to India in _____ during its war with China",
      "answer": "1962",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The Republic of India operates a High Commission in Port of Spain, whilst the Republic of Trinidad and Tobago operates a High Commission in New Delhi.  United States of America Before and during the Second World War, the United States under President Roosevelt gave strong support to the Indian independence movement despite being allies to Britain.  Relations between India and the United States were lukewarm following Indian independence, as India took a leading position in the Non-Aligned Movement, and received support from the Soviet Union.  The US provided support to India in 1962 during its war with China.  For most of the Cold War, the USA tended to have warmer relations with Pakistan, primarily as a way to contain Soviet-friendly India and to use Pakistan to back the Afghan Mujahideen against the Soviet occupation of Afghanistan.  An Indo-Soviet Treaty of Friendship and Cooperation, signed in 1971, also positioned India against the USA.  After the Sino-Indian War and the Indo-Pakistani War of 1965, India made considerable changes to its foreign policy"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2612",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
