      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "Foreign relations of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "However, India has enjoyed a favourable relationship with Bangladesh during governments formed by the Awami League in _____ and 1996",
      "answer": "1972",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Bangladesh India was the second country to recognise Bangladesh as a separate and independent state, doing so on 6 December 1971.  India fought alongside the Bangladeshis to liberate Bangladesh from West Pakistan in 1971.  Bangladesh's relationship with India has been difficult in terms of border killing, irrigation and land border disputes post-1976.  However, India has enjoyed a favourable relationship with Bangladesh during governments formed by the Awami League in 1972 and 1996.  The recent solutions to land and maritime disputes have taken out irritants in ties.  At the outset, India's relations with Bangladesh could not have been stronger because of India's unalloyed support for independence and opposition against Pakistan in 1971.  During the independence war, many refugees fled to India"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2631",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:30:18.807Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "Foreign relations of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "India extended recognition to Bangladesh before the end of the war in _____ (the second country to do so after Bhutan) and subsequently lobbied others to follow suit",
      "answer": "1971",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " During the independence war, many refugees fled to India.  When the struggle of resistance matured in November 1971, India also intervened militarily and may have helped bring international attention to the issue through Indira Gandhi's visit to Washington, D.  Afterwards India furnished relief and reconstruction aid.  India extended recognition to Bangladesh before the end of the war in 1971 (the second country to do so after Bhutan) and subsequently lobbied others to follow suit.  India also withdrew its military from the land of Bangladesh when Sheikh Mujibur Rahman requested Indira Gandhi to do so during the latter's visit to Dhaka in 1972.  Indo-Bangladesh relations have been somewhat less friendly since the fall of the Mujib government in August 1975.  over the years over issues such as South Talpatti Island, the Tin Bigha Corridor and access to Nepal, the Farakka Barrage and water sharing, border conflicts near Tripura and the construction of a fence along most of the border which India explains as security provision against migrants, insurgents and terrorists"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2632",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:30:18.808Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "Foreign relations of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Bilateral relations warmed in _____, due to a softer Indian foreign policy and the new Awami League Government",
      "answer": "1996",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Indo-Bangladesh relations have been somewhat less friendly since the fall of the Mujib government in August 1975.  over the years over issues such as South Talpatti Island, the Tin Bigha Corridor and access to Nepal, the Farakka Barrage and water sharing, border conflicts near Tripura and the construction of a fence along most of the border which India explains as security provision against migrants, insurgents and terrorists.  Many Bangladeshis feel India likes to play \"big brother\" to smaller neighbours, including Bangladesh.  Bilateral relations warmed in 1996, due to a softer Indian foreign policy and the new Awami League Government.  A 30-year water-sharing agreement for the Ganges River was signed in December 1996, after an earlier bilateral water-sharing agreement for the Ganges River lapsed in 1988.  Both nations also have cooperated on the issue of flood warnings and preparedness.  The Bangladesh Government and tribal insurgents signed a peace accord in December 1997, which allowed for the return of tribal refugees who had fled to India, beginning in 1986, to escape violence caused by an insurgency in their homeland in the Chittagong Hill Tracts"
    },
    {
      "id": "q2633",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "International Relations",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:30:18.809Z",
      "subject": "International Relations",
      "subSubject": "Foreign relations of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "A 30-year water-sharing agreement for the Ganges River was signed in December _____, after an earlier bilateral water-sharing agreement for the Ganges River lapsed in 1988",
      "answer": "1996",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " over the years over issues such as South Talpatti Island, the Tin Bigha Corridor and access to Nepal, the Farakka Barrage and water sharing, border conflicts near Tripura and the construction of a fence along most of the border which India explains as security provision against migrants, insurgents and terrorists.  Many Bangladeshis feel India likes to play \"big brother\" to smaller neighbours, including Bangladesh.  Bilateral relations warmed in 1996, due to a softer Indian foreign policy and the new Awami League Government.  A 30-year water-sharing agreement for the Ganges River was signed in December 1996, after an earlier bilateral water-sharing agreement for the Ganges River lapsed in 1988.  Both nations also have cooperated on the issue of flood warnings and preparedness.  The Bangladesh Government and tribal insurgents signed a peace accord in December 1997, which allowed for the return of tribal refugees who had fled to India, beginning in 1986, to escape violence caused by an insurgency in their homeland in the Chittagong Hill Tracts.  The Bangladesh Army maintains a very strong presence in the area to this day"
    },
