      "emoji": "",
      "question": "Despite the demands to have him removed and suspended, Varma was transferred to the _____, sparking a backlash from lawyers and other judges",
      "answer": "Allahabad High Court",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Nepotism – wherein favors are sought and dispensed by some judges.  In March 2025, large bundles of cash, some burnt, were found in the house of Delhi High Court Judge Yashwant Varma by the fire department, after a fire was reported at his house, reigniting a major controversy on judicial corruption.  The Supreme Court declined to hear a plea seeking an FIR against Varma following the expose.  Despite the demands to have him removed and suspended, Varma was transferred to the Allahabad High Court, sparking a backlash from lawyers and other judges.  An impeachment process against Verma is due to be initiated in the Parliament monsoon season in July 2025.  On 26 February 2026, the Supreme Court slammed the NCERT, after a class 8 social science textbook revealed a section on corruption in judiciary.  Calling it a deep-rooted conspiracy to defame and undermine the judiciary, the Chief Justice Surya Kant, in a suo motu case hearing, called the move to teach pupils about judicial corruption and pendency of cases as reckless, and ordered that the books should not be issued to students"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3531",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Constitution",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:30:32.145Z",
      "subject": "Constitution",
      "subSubject": "Judiciary of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "On 26 February 2026, the _____ slammed the NCERT, after a class 8 social science textbook revealed a section on corruption in judiciary",
      "answer": "Supreme Court",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The Supreme Court declined to hear a plea seeking an FIR against Varma following the expose.  Despite the demands to have him removed and suspended, Varma was transferred to the Allahabad High Court, sparking a backlash from lawyers and other judges.  An impeachment process against Verma is due to be initiated in the Parliament monsoon season in July 2025.  On 26 February 2026, the Supreme Court slammed the NCERT, after a class 8 social science textbook revealed a section on corruption in judiciary.  Calling it a deep-rooted conspiracy to defame and undermine the judiciary, the Chief Justice Surya Kant, in a suo motu case hearing, called the move to teach pupils about judicial corruption and pendency of cases as reckless, and ordered that the books should not be issued to students.  === Nepotism === Uncle judge syndrome is a nepotistic phenomenon in the Indian judiciary where the collegium system, the opaque system of which the incumbent judges of the Supreme Court of India appoint new judges, is misused to appoint family and relatives of the judges.  In 2017, the Supreme Court had recognised the rot of the collegium, but as of 2025 little has been done to fix it"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3532",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Constitution",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:30:32.145Z",
      "subject": "Constitution",
      "subSubject": "Judiciary of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "In 2017, the _____ had recognised the rot of the collegium, but as of 2025 little has been done to fix it",
      "answer": "Supreme Court",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " On 26 February 2026, the Supreme Court slammed the NCERT, after a class 8 social science textbook revealed a section on corruption in judiciary.  Calling it a deep-rooted conspiracy to defame and undermine the judiciary, the Chief Justice Surya Kant, in a suo motu case hearing, called the move to teach pupils about judicial corruption and pendency of cases as reckless, and ordered that the books should not be issued to students.  === Nepotism === Uncle judge syndrome is a nepotistic phenomenon in the Indian judiciary where the collegium system, the opaque system of which the incumbent judges of the Supreme Court of India appoint new judges, is misused to appoint family and relatives of the judges.  In 2017, the Supreme Court had recognised the rot of the collegium, but as of 2025 little has been done to fix it.  In 2015, a research by advocate Mathews J Nedumpara claims that around 50% of the High Court judges and 33% of the Supreme Court judges are family members of those in higher echelons of judiciary, with six Supreme Court judges were sons of former judges and over 88 High Court judges were either born to a family of lawyers, judges, or worked under some legal luminaries.  Wh"
    },
    {
      "id": "q3533",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Constitution",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:30:32.146Z",
      "subject": "Constitution",
      "subSubject": "Judiciary of India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "However, in October 2015, the Act and the Constitutional amendment was struck down by the _____ of the Supreme Court",
      "answer": "Constitution Bench",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " In 2015, a research by advocate Mathews J Nedumpara claims that around 50% of the High Court judges and 33% of the Supreme Court judges are family members of those in higher echelons of judiciary, with six Supreme Court judges were sons of former judges and over 88 High Court judges were either born to a family of lawyers, judges, or worked under some legal luminaries.  While in 2022, former Minister of State for Human Resource Development Upendra Kushwaha noted that judges of Supreme Court so far have come only from 250-300 families, and there is negligible representation of women and Scheduled Castes in the higher judiciary.  The Parliament of India attempted to abolish the collegium system in 2014 by establishing the National Judicial Appointments Commission through the National Judicial Appointments Commission Act, 2014 and the Constitution (99th Amendment) Act, 2014.  However, in October 2015, the Act and the Constitutional amendment was struck down by the Constitution Bench of the Supreme Court.  Jasti Chelameswar, a Supreme Court judge, who was a part of the NJAC verdict of 2015, gave a dissenting opinion, stating that the NJAC could have acted “as a check on unwholesome tr"
    },
