      "emoji": "",
      "question": "_____, in his review of caste in India, states that anyone could in principle perform any profession",
      "answer": "Peter Masefield",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " This class was apparently not defined by birth, but by individual economic growth.  While there was an alignment between kulas and occupations at least at the high and low ends, there was no strict linkage between class/caste and occupation, especially among those in the middle range.  Many occupations listed such as accounting and writing were not linked to jatis.  Peter Masefield, in his review of caste in India, states that anyone could in principle perform any profession.  The texts state that the Brahmin took food from anyone, suggesting that strictures of commensality were as yet unknown.  The Nikaya texts also imply that endogamy was not mandated.  The contestations of the period are also evident from the texts describing dialogues of Buddha with the Brahmins"
    },
