      "question": "=== _____ === ==== Caste politics ==== Societal stratification, and the inequality that comes with it, still exists in India, and has been thoroughly criticised",
      "answer": "Contemporary India",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The emergence of a caste system in the modern form, during the early period of British colonial rule in the 18th and 19th centuries, was not uniform in South Asia.  Claude Markovits, a French historian of colonial India, writes that Hindu society in north and west India (Sindh), in the late 18th century and much of the 19th century, lacked a proper caste system, their religious identities were fluid (a combination of Saivism, Vaisnavism, Sikhism), and the Brahmins were not the widespread priestly group (but the Bawas were).  Markovits writes, \"if religion was not a structuring factor, neither was caste\" among the Hindu merchants group of northwest India.  === Contemporary India === ==== Caste politics ==== Societal stratification, and the inequality that comes with it, still exists in India, and has been thoroughly criticised.  Government policies aim at reducing this inequality by reservation – a quota system benefitting backward classes.  The Indian government officially recognises historically discriminated communities of India such as the untouchables under the designation of Scheduled Castes, and certain economically backward castes as Other Backward Class.  Some, such as soc"
    },
