      "question": "There is history of skin color defining Pak/Paleed, but that does not have historical roots, and was adopted by outsiders through analogy with the _____ system",
      "answer": "Hindu Caste",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " Qaums have patrilineal heredity, with ranked occupations and endogamy.  Membership in a qaum is inherited by birth.  Barth identifies the origin of the stratification from the historical segregation between pak (pure) and paleed (impure)—defined by the family's social or religious status, occupation and involvement in sexual crimes.  There is history of skin color defining Pak/Paleed, but that does not have historical roots, and was adopted by outsiders through analogy with the Hindu Caste system.  Endogamy is very common in the form of arranged consanguineous marriages among Muslims in India and Pakistan.  Malik states that the lack of religious sanction makes qaum a \"quasi-caste\", and something that is found in Islam outside South Asia as well.  === Sikh === The Sikh literature mentions Varna as Varan, and Jati as Zat or Zat-biradari"
