      "question": "_____ mobile applications for reporting sanitation issues and tracking progress under the Swachh Bharat Mission",
      "answer": "Swachh Bharat Mission",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " eSign, which enables citizens to sign electronic documents online using Aadhaar‑based authentication.  e‑Hospital, which provides online registration, appointment booking, fee payment, and access to diagnostic reports in participating government hospitals.  MyGov, a citizen engagement platform for consultations, feedback, and crowdsourcing of ideas.  Swachh Bharat Mission mobile applications for reporting sanitation issues and tracking progress under the Swachh Bharat Mission. in, an Aadhaar‑enabled biometric attendance system for government employees.  Sector‑specific platforms associated with Digital India include: DIKSHA, the Digital Infrastructure for Knowledge Sharing, which provides curriculum‑aligned digital content and teacher resources for school education; eSanjeevani, a national telemedicine service offering doctor‑to‑doctor and doctor‑to‑patient tele‑consultations; CoWIN, a platform used for managing COVID‑19 vaccination appointments and certificates; e‑NAM (National Agriculture Market), which facilitates online trading of agricultural commodities through integrated markets.  === Back-end digitisation === Alongside citizen‑facing applications, Digital India encompasses"
    },
    {
      "id": "q4837",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Tech & Science",
      "region": "",
      "source": "Wiki",
      "pubDate": "2026-06-28T13:33:35.285Z",
      "subject": "Tech & Science",
      "subSubject": "Digital India",
      "emoji": "",
      "question": "The programme has coincided with the growth of Indian edtech companies including Byju’s, Unacademy, Vedantu, _____, and others that deliver online learning and test preparation",
      "answer": "Physics Wallah",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": " The rise of fintech and digital commerce has been closely linked to Digital India.  Companies such as Paytm, PhonePe, Google Pay, Razorpay, and BharatPe offer payment applications and merchant solutions built on UPI and related systems, extending digital payments to small merchants and consumers across urban and rural markets.  Government‑backed platforms such as Government e‑Marketplace (GeM) and the Open Network for Digital Commerce (ONDC) seek to digitise procurement and retail, including participation by micro, small and medium enterprises (MSMEs).  The programme has coincided with the growth of Indian edtech companies including Byju’s, Unacademy, Vedantu, Physics Wallah, and others that deliver online learning and test preparation.  These platforms complement public initiatives such as DIKSHA and PM eVIDYA, although issues of affordability, quality, and equitable access have been noted in research on India’s edtech sector.  Industry reports by NASSCOM and startup trackers identify hundreds of Indian artificial intelligence and automation startups working in fields such as language technologies, computer vision, enterprise automation, industrial internet of things (IIoT), and "
    },
    {
      "id": "q4838",
      "type": "fill_blank",
      "category": "Ethics",
