      "hint": "",
      "fact": " This was one of the three banks founded by a presidency government, the other two were the Bank of Bombay in 1840 and the Bank of Madras in 1843.  The three banks were merged in 1921 to form the Imperial Bank of India, which upon India's independence, became the State Bank of India in 1955.  For many years, the presidency banks had acted as quasi-central banks, as did their successors, until the Reserve Bank of India was established in 1935, under the Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934.  In 1960, the State Banks of India was given control of eight state-associated banks under the State Bank of India (Subsidiary Banks) Act, 1959.  However the merger of these associated banks with SBI went into effect on 1 April 2017.  In 1969, the Government of India nationalised 14 major private banks; one of the big banks was Bank of India.  In 1980, 6 more private banks were nationalised"
