      "question": "What is Thermal effect of field emission?",
      "answer": "Nottingham effect",
      "hint": "",
      "fact": "In condensed matter physics, the Nottingham effect is a surface cooling and heating mechanism that occurs during field and thermionic electron emission. The effect is named after physicist Wayne B. Nottingham who explained it in a commentary to 1940 experiments by Gertrude M. Fleming and Joseph E. Henderson. The temperature at which electron emission goes from heating to cooling is called the Nottingham inversion temperature."
